    "evidence_text": "Here we tested these hypotheses in haematopoietic stem cells (HSCs), which can be highly purified using well characterized markers.\n\nHematopoietic stem cell: self-renewal versus differentiation.\n\nThe mammalian blood system, containing more than 10 distinct mature cell types, stands on one specific cell type, hematopoietic stem cell (HSC).\n\nAsymmetric Cell Divisions Sustain Long-Term  Hematopoiesis from Single-sorted Human Fetal Liver Cells \n\nSuccessful ex vivo expansion of hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs) would greatly benefit the treatment of disease and the understanding of crucial questions of stem cell biology.\n\nPurification and characterization of mouse hematopoietic stem cells.\n\nMouse bone marrow hematopoietic stem cells were isolated with the use of a variety of phenotypic markers.\n\nThese cells can proliferate and differentiate with approximately unit efficiency into myelomonocytic cells, B cells, or T cells.\n\nThirty of these cells are sufficient to save 50 percent of lethally irradiated mice, and to reconstitute all blood cell types in the survivors.\n\nHES-1 preserves purified hematopoietic stem cells ex vivo and accumulates side population cells in vivo.",
